        {
          "ruleId": "pmat/dead-code-added-by-this-diff",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Three definitions added by this diff have zero call sites. install.sh defines info() at line 100 and never calls it (grep for an `info \"...\"` invocation returns 0, exit 1). install.sh assigns DIM at line 47 and clears it at line 61 and never expands it ($DIM / ${DIM} match nowhere, exit 1) - it is the only one of the fourteen colour variables with no reference; the other thirteen are referenced at lines 77-112 and 288. install_test.sh defines the t() row helper at line 111 and never calls it: every row goes through t_grep() or is spelled out inline, so the n=$((n+1)) at line 118 is unreachable."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "scripts/install.sh"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 100
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "measured",
            "command": [
              "grep",
              "-c",
              "-E",
              "(^|[^[:alnum:]_])info[[:space:]]+\"",
              "scripts/install.sh"
            ],
            "exit_code": 1,
            "stdout_sha256": "9a271f2a916b0b6ee6cecb2426f0b3206ef074578be55d9bc94f6f3fe3ab86aa",
            "precision_class": "advisory",
            "corroborating_commands": [
              [
                "grep",
                "-c",
                "-E",
                "^[[:space:]]*t[[:space:]]+[0-9]",
                "scripts/tests/install_test.sh"
              ],
              [
                "grep",
                "-n",
                "-E",
                "\\$\\{?DIM\\}?",
                "scripts/install.sh"
              ]
            ],
            "corroborating_results": {
              "t()_call_sites": 0,
              "$DIM_references": 0
            },
            "failure_scenario": "t() is the more expensive of the three: it is the only helper that asserts an exit code WITHOUT also asserting output, so a maintainer adding an exit-code-only row will reach for it, and it has never been executed once. Its n=$((n+1)) runs in the caller's shell, so the first use also silently shifts every subsequent row number in the report."
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "pmat/hardcoded-row-count-never-matched-the-code",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "install_test.sh:177 prints 'skipping the 6 network-dependent rows below' from a hardcoded literal 6. The network branch (lines 178-309) holds 9 rows - measured by counting t_grep calls plus n=$((n+1)) increments in that range, which returns 9. The literal was never right: at the first commit of this branch (179c69b2f) the same branch held 8. The PR body's test plan likewise still reads '13/13 rows pass' while the suite at this head runs 14 (the CI job log for run 35115773219 ends 'install_test.sh: 14 row(s), all ok'); commit 9132c9408 added the 'default (no tty, no flags) stays plain' row without updating either count."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "scripts/tests/install_test.sh"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 177
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "measured",
            "command": [
              "bash",
              "-c",
              "sed -n '178,309p' scripts/tests/install_test.sh | grep -cE '^ *(t_grep |n=\\$\\(\\(n \\+ 1\\)\\))'"
            ],
            "exit_code": 0,
            "stdout_sha256": "2e6d31a5983a91251bfae5aefa1c0a19d8ba3cf601d0e8a706b4cfa9661a6b8a",
            "precision_class": "advisory",
            "counts": {
              "literal_in_message": 6,
              "actual_network_rows_at_head": 9,
              "actual_network_rows_at_179c69b2f": 8,
              "total_rows_at_head": 14,
              "pr_body_claims": 13
            },
            "failure_scenario": "The skip message is the ONLY signal a reader gets that rows vanished (the suite still exits 0 - see install-test/vacuous-green-when-github-unreachable). A reader who trusts it believes 6 of 14 rows were skipped and 8 ran, when in fact 9 were skipped and 5 ran. CLAUDE.md's standing rule is to derive counts rather than quote them, and this is a quoted one that has drifted twice already in a five-commit branch."
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "pmat/cuda-asset-name-never-exercised",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "install-script.yml's header states the gate exists because 'install.sh assumes a specific asset-naming shape from binary-release.yml (stable: apr-vX.Y.Z-<target>-<cpu|cuda>.tar.gz)'. No row ever fetches a -cuda asset: --cuda and APR_VARIANT=cuda appear 0 times in install_test.sh, while --cpu is forced in 8 places. The one row that does not force --cpu is the 404 row (line 179, --version v0.0.1-does-not-exist-3365), which takes whatever detect_variant() returns on the runner - on this reviewer's box, which has a working nvidia-smi, the instrumented run fetched .../apr-v0.0.1-does-not-exist-3365-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-cuda.tar.gz, so which half of the naming contract that row exercises is a property of the runner, not of the test."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "scripts/tests/install_test.sh"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 179
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "measured",
            "command": [
              "grep",
              "-c",
              "-E",
              "--",
              "--cuda|APR_VARIANT=cuda",
              "scripts/tests/install_test.sh"
            ],
            "exit_code": 1,
            "stdout_sha256": "9a271f2a916b0b6ee6cecb2426f0b3206ef074578be55d9bc94f6f3fe3ab86aa",
            "precision_class": "advisory",
            "corroborating_commands": [
              [
                "bash",
                "-c",
                "sort <curl-shim call log from one instrumented full run> | uniq -c; curl -fsSL https://api.github.com/rate_limit | jq -c .resources.core"
              ]
            ],
            "failure_scenario": "binary-release.yml renames or drops only the cuda half of the stable asset naming (for example apr-vX.Y.Z-<target>-cuda -> apr-vX.Y.Z-<target>-gpu). Every row stays green because every row that downloads successfully has passed --cpu, and the first report is a CUDA user's `curl | sh` failing at the download step - which is precisely the class of drift the workflow header says this gate exists to catch early."
          }
        }
      ]
