        {
          "ruleId": "mutants/checksum-verification-is-untested-surviving-mutant",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "SURVIVING MUTANT. install_test.sh's header names the checksum row as one of two rows that exist because the failure already happened: 'the one failure mode where \"install succeeded\" and \"installed a corrupt binary\" must never be confused'. The row (line 302-308) does not exercise that path - it runs `grep -qE 'Checksum mismatch.*not installing' \"$SCRIPT\"` against the script's own source text. Mutant: change line 245 from `expected=$(awk '{print $1}' \"$tmp_dir/archive.tar.gz.sha256\")` to `expected=$(sha256_of \"$tmp_dir/archive.tar.gz\")`, so `expected` is computed from the very file being verified and can never mismatch, while every character of the fatal wording stays. Measured against that mutant: row 14's grep still matches, the five network-free rows still report ok, and a real `--version v0.67.0 --cpu` install through the mutant exits 0 and produces a working `apr 0.67.0`. No row turns red."
          },
